# Local development. Copy this file to `.env`:  cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is the ONLY local environment file. The Prisma CLI loads it natively
# and `node --env-file=.env` loads it for the API; a second `.env.local` would
# be a second way to be wrong. apps/api/test/env-example.test.ts asserts in
# CI that this file is a superset of what apps/api's Zod schema requires, so
# a fresh clone can never fail with an unexplained missing-variable error —
# that gate is enforced, not a manual discipline; letting this file drift
# fails `pnpm verify`.

NODE_ENV=development

# --- API ---
API_PORT=3001
LOG_LEVEL=debug
# Guards /health/deep, which reports per-dependency latency. Replaced by the
# Clerk guard in Phase 1; until then a shared secret is the control, and
# apps/api/test/health.integration.test.ts asserts the 401.
#
# The schema enforces a 16-character MINIMUM and nothing else — it cannot
# measure entropy, so `aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa` passes validation and is not a secret.
# Outside local development, generate it: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
HEALTH_DEEP_TOKEN=local-health-deep-token

# --- API telemetry ---
# Where apps/api sends error events. Empty is the LOCAL default: `Sentry.init()`
# still runs, and the shared TracerProvider, the sampler and the propagator are
# all still Sentry's, so correlation and tracing behave identically. What an empty
# value does NOT leave running is described below, and it is more than a transport.
#
# A DSN is not a secret — apps/web compiles its own into every visitor's bundle
# by design. This one is server-side only and carries no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# EMPTY DISABLES MORE THAN THE TRANSPORT. @sentry/node does not construct any
# integration at all for a client with no DSN, so a local run with this empty is
# not exercising the Sentry half of the bootstrap in any respect — see ADR-0034,
# which exists because a measurement was taken in exactly that state.
SENTRY_DSN=
# The FULL OTLP/HTTP traces endpoint, path included — `http://localhost:4318/v1/
# traces`, never a bare origin. Deliberately not named OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT:
# that variable belongs to the OpenTelemetry SDK, which reads it itself and
# appends the signal path, and two meanings under one name is how an endpoint
# ends up one segment away from a collector.
#
# Empty means no exporter is constructed at all, which is what a developer
# without a collector wants. Correlation is unaffected — requestId, traceId and
# spanId come from the live trace context, not from the exporter — so an empty
# value costs the exported trace and nothing else. `pnpm infra:up` does not start
# a collector today; apps/workers carries the same switch as
# METRIKA_WORKER_OTLP_ENDPOINT.
OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT=
# Head-based sampling for BOTH sinks: it reaches OpenTelemetry through
# SentrySampler, which is the single sampler on the shared provider. `0` does not
# mean "correlation only" — a dropped span still has a valid trace ID, so log
# lines keep traceId and spanId while nothing is ever exported.
#
# `1` is the schema default too, so a deployment that omits this samples
# everything. That is inert while neither sink above is configured and is a
# decision to make at the moment one of them is.
#
# FOR A W3C-ONLY CALLER IT IS A FLOOR, NOT A CEILING. Measured at 0: a request
# arriving with `traceparent: …-01` still exports its spans in full, because a
# caller's SET sampled flag is inherited ahead of this value; only a CLEARED flag
# (`-00`) falls through to this rate.
#
# ON SENTRY'S OWN HEADER THE CALLER DECIDES OUTRIGHT, BOTH WAYS, and it beats an
# inbound `traceparent` either way. Measured: `sentry-trace: …-0` exports ZERO
# spans at a rate of 1, `sentry-trace: …-1` exports in full at a rate of 0.
#
# Those were curl-supplied headers, and NOTHING HERE RESTRICTS WHO MAY SEND THEM
# — whoever does decides this API's sampling for that trace. It is NOT our own
# browser today, and that correction is measured rather than assumed: apps/web
# ships @sentry/nextjs but drops BrowserTracing from its integration allowlist,
# and a fetch from the shipped client configuration carries neither
# `sentry-trace` nor `baggage`, while the same fetch under the SDK's defaults
# carries both. See ADR-0035 for the eight cells, ADR-0036 for this correction.
TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=1

# --- Web ---
# The port `next start` and `next dev` bind to. Read differently from API_PORT
# above, and the difference is worth knowing: apps/web's scripts expand it in
# the SHELL (`next start --port ${WEB_PORT:-3000}`), so it takes effect only if
# it is exported into the environment that runs the script. Setting it here
# alone still changes nothing, and it is worth knowing that this is now true for
# a second, independent reason.
#
# Four root scripts — `build`, `dev`, `test:unit`, `test:integration` — go
# through `scripts/turbo.mjs` with `--env-file-if-exists=.env`, because every
# one of them ends up running `next build` or `next dev`, and both resolve the
# two NEXT_PUBLIC_ keys below. So this file IS loaded for them. But turbo runs
# in strict env mode and filters each task's environment down to what
# `turbo.json` declares: MEASURED, a task declaring no env sees both
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ keys (turbo infers them from the `nextjs` framework) and does NOT
# see WEB_PORT. Declaring it would be the fix, if a non-default port through
# turbo were ever wanted.
#
# `pnpm --filter @metrika/web start` bypasses all of that and loads nothing, so
# a non-default port for `next start` has to be exported by hand either way.
#
# Listed anyway, and not aspirationally: ServerEnvSchema declares it, and
# apps/web/test/env-example.test.ts fails if a key that schema declares is
# missing from this file. When apps/web starts calling loadServerEnv() this
# entry becomes live with no edit here.
WEB_PORT=3000

# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A KEY HERE. `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is not a naming
# convention: Next replaces the literal text `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_<NAME>`
# with the value at BUILD time, so the value is compiled into the JavaScript
# every visitor downloads. It is public, permanently, to anyone who opens
# devtools — and it is baked into the bundle, so rotating it needs a rebuild and
# a redeploy, not a restart. Never put a secret behind this prefix. A key with
# no `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix is server-only and never reaches the browser; that is
# where anything sensitive goes.
#
# Where apps/web's API client points. Same origin the API serves on (API_PORT
# above), because the browser is what calls it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001
# The locale used until the visitor's own preference is known. One of the two
# in SUPPORTED_LOCALES — declared in apps/web/src/i18n/routing.ts and
# re-exported from apps/web/src/config/env.ts: es-CO or en-US.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEFAULT_LOCALE=es-CO
# Where apps/web's browser and server Sentry SDKs send errors.
#
# A DSN is not a secret — it is compiled into every visitor's bundle by design,
# which is why it carries the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix. Empty here is the LOCAL
# default, so a developer without a Sentry project is not blocked; a deployed
# environment sets it.
#
# It used to be empty for a second and stronger reason, and that reason is
# discharged: apps/api's exception filter wrote an Error's stack, and a stack
# begins with its message, so a DSN or a database URL in a thrown message was
# written down — which made a populated sink a decision Plan 0C had ordered
# behind the redaction work. Task 2 closed it (apps/api/src/infrastructure/
# telemetry/redaction.ts + logger.ts, asserted in apps/api/test/logger.test.ts
# and error-filter.test.ts). METRIKA_WORKER_OTLP_ENDPOINT below is still
# commented out, but on its own grounds rather than on this one.
#
# Empty is the ONLY value that disables it. Anything else must be a real DSN:
# ClientEnvSchema rejects a malformed one at build time rather than letting
# Sentry log "invalid DSN" at runtime and report nothing for a month.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# --- Database ---
# Two URLs, two roles, deliberately. The API connects as metrika_app, which is
# NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS so row-level security actually applies to it.
# Migrations connect as the owner. Since Prisma 7 the owner's URL is named by
# packages/database/prisma.config.ts, NOT by schema.prisma — a datasource `url`
# in a schema file is rejected outright on 7, and the config file resolves
# env('DATABASE_ADMIN_URL') for every subcommand, `generate` included.
#
# No `?schema=public`. Prisma 7 talks to Postgres through the `pg` driver
# adapter, which never sees that parameter — it is now inert, and a parameter
# that looks load-bearing and is not is worse than an absent one. Select a
# non-public schema with `new PrismaPg(url, { schema })`; see ADR-0037 and
# packages/database/test/adapter.integration.test.ts.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://metrika_app:metrika_app@localhost:5432/metrika_dev
DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgresql://metrika:metrika@localhost:5432/metrika_dev

# --- Dependencies present in docker compose, wired up in later plans ---
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_BUCKET=metrika-local
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=metrika
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=metrika-local
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
SMTP_URL=smtp://localhost:1025

# --- apps/workers (Python) ---
# `METRIKA_WORKER_`, not `METRIKA_`, and the prefix is load-bearing rather than
# decorative. `metrika_core.WorkerSettings` sets `extra="forbid"` and reads the
# WHOLE namespace, so an unrecognised variable in it is a startup error — which
# is the point for a typo, and would be hostile over a prefix somebody else
# writes to. `METRIKA_` is exactly that: packages/testing publishes
# `METRIKA_TEST_DATABASE_URL` and `METRIKA_TEST_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL`, and under
# the wider prefix a shell that had run the Node integration harness could not
# construct worker settings at all.
#
# THERE IS NO DATABASE VARIABLE HERE AND THERE MUST NEVER BE ONE. A worker has
# no database credentials (ADR-0007); it receives its inputs as Temporal
# activity arguments and reaches S3 under a prefix-scoped role.
# `metrika_core/tests/test_settings.py` asserts the exact field set, and
# `test_dependencies.py` asserts the package cannot even install a driver.
METRIKA_WORKER_TEMPORAL_ADDRESS=localhost:7233
METRIKA_WORKER_TEMPORAL_NAMESPACE=default
METRIKA_WORKER_TEMPORAL_TASK_QUEUE=geometry-small
METRIKA_WORKER_S3_BUCKET=metrika-local
# Unset against real AWS. When set, the client switches to path-style
# addressing, because MinIO has no wildcard DNS for virtual-hosted buckets.
METRIKA_WORKER_S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:9000
# debug | info | warning | error | critical — a Literal, so a typo is a
# ValidationError naming the field rather than a KeyError inside the logger.
METRIKA_WORKER_LOG_LEVEL=info
# Where OpenTelemetry spans go. COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE: unset means no
# exporter is constructed at all, which is what a developer without a collector
# wants. The second reason it carried — that no task may ship a populated sink
# before apps/api's log redaction lands — is discharged as of Plan 0C Task 2;
# what keeps it commented out now is that nothing in this repository constructs
# a span yet. The correlation fields still reach every log line without it —
# they come from the live trace context, not from the exporter.
#
# No credential beside it. The OTLP exporter reads OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS
# from the environment itself, so an authentication token never becomes a field
# on WorkerSettings — the same reasoning as the AWS note below.
# METRIKA_WORKER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318/v1/traces
# Credentials and region are deliberately NOT worker settings: they come from
# the standard AWS provider chain (AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY /
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION locally, an IRSA or instance role in production), so there
# is no field on WorkerSettings that could hold one.
